Let $h\in\mathbb Z[x]$ be prime-intersective, of degree at least two and with positive leading coefficient. There exist constants $c_h>0$ and $C_h\ge1$ such that, for every positive integer $N$ and every $A\subseteq[N]$, $$(A-A)\cap\{h(p):p\in\mathbb P\}\subseteq\{0\}
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 |A|\le C_h N^{1-c_h}.$$

For every $l\ge1$, the polynomial $h_l$ belongs to $\mathbb Z[x]$, has degree $k$ and positive leading coefficient $a_l$, and all its coefficients are $O_h(a_l)$. We have $h_1=h$ and $T_1(x)=x$. For every $D\ge1$, there is an integer $i\in\{0,\ldots,D-1\}$ such that $$\label{eq:auxiliary-change}
 \begin{split}
 T_l(Dx-i)&=T_{lD}(x),\\
 h_l(Dx-i)&=\lambda(D)h_{lD}(x),\qquad
 D\mid\lambda(D),\quad \lambda(D)\le D^k.
 \end{split}$$ Moreover, $b_{m_p,p}$ is a $p$-adic unit for all sufficiently large $p$, with a threshold depending only on $h$.

There is an integer $P_1>\max(k,2)$, depending only on $h$, such that for every $l\ge1$ and every prime $p\ge P_1$, at least $p/2$ arguments in $\mathbb F_p$ are allowed regular. For every integer $v$ with $p\nmid v$, $$\label{eq:local-sums}
 \begin{split}
 \left|\mathbb E_{\substack{x\bmod p^j\\p\nmid T_l(x)}}
 e\left(\frac{vF(x)}{p^j}\right)\right|
 &\le p^{-8\delta j} \qquad(j\ge1),\\
 \left|\mathbb E_{\substack{x\bmod p\\x\text{ allowed regular}}}
 e\left(\frac{vF(x)}p\right)\right|
 &\le p^{-4\delta}.
 \end{split}$$ Each expectation is normalized by the number of arguments in its stated averaging set.

For every prime $p<P_1$, there is an integer $S_p\ge0$, depending only on $h$ and $p$, such that, for every $l\ge1$, some integer $x$ satisfies $$\label{eq:small-prime-argument}
 p\nmid T_l(x),\qquad v_p(h_l'(x))\le S_p.$$ Set $$\label{eq:local-modulus-exponents}
 E_p=2S_p+1\quad(p<P_1),\qquad E_p=1\quad(p\ge P_1).$$ For any prime $p$, let $x$ be an integer satisfying <a href="#eq:small-prime-argument" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:small-prime-argument">[eq:small-prime-argument]</a> when $p<P_1$, or representing an allowed regular argument when $p\ge P_1$. Write $s=v_p(h_l'(x))$. Every integer in the class $x\pmod{p^{E_p-s}}$ satisfies the unit condition, and its $h_l$-value is congruent to $h_l(x)$ modulo $p^{E_p}$. For each $j>E_p$, the uniform distribution on this argument class modulo $p^j$ pushes forward under $h_l$ to the uniform distribution on $$\{y\bmod p^j:y\equiv h_l(x)\pmod{p^{E_p}}\}.$$

There is a threshold $P>P_1$, depending only on $h$, with the following property. For every $l\ge1$ and prime $p\ge P$, there are a probability measure $\mu_{l,p}$ on $\mathbb F_p^V$ and a submeasure $0\le\varepsilon_{l,p}\le\mu_{l,p}$ such that:*

1.  *$\mu_{l,p}$ is reflection positive. Both $\mu_{l,p}$ and $\varepsilon_{l,p}$ are invariant under simultaneous translation of all labels. In particular, any nonzero one of these measures, or their difference, has uniform single-slot marginals after normalization.*

2.  *Writing $$\eta_{l,p}=\varepsilon_{l,p}(1),\qquad
     \mu_{l,p}^{\circ}=\frac{\mu_{l,p}-\varepsilon_{l,p}}{1-\eta_{l,p}},$$ we have $\eta_{l,p}\le p^{-4}$. Under $\mu_{l,p}^{\circ}$ every increment $z_{v_{j+1}}-z_{v_j}$, $0\le j<L$, is admissible at $l$.*


3.  *For any integer $D\ge1$ with $p\nmid D$ and any $a'\in\mathbb F_p$, let $A_{D,a'}$ act on all labels by $z_v\mapsto\lambda(D)^{-1}(z_v-a')$. Then $$\label{eq:tuple-covariance}
     (A_{D,a'})_*\mu_{l,p}=\mu_{lD,p},\qquad
     (A_{D,a'})_*\varepsilon_{l,p}=\varepsilon_{lD,p}.$$*


4.  *For $\rho_p=\mu_{l,p}$ or $\mu_{l,p}^{\circ}$, every $v\in V$, and every $f:\mathbb F_p\to\mathbb C$ of uniform mean zero, $$\label{eq:tuple-mixing}
     \left\|\mathbb E_{\rho_p}
       \bigl[f(z_v)\mid(z_u)_{u\ne v}\bigr]\right\|_{L^2(\rho_p)}
     \le p^{-\gamma}\|f\|_{L^2(\mathbb F_p)}.$$*

*All thresholds and constants are independent of $l$.

Let $G$ be a fixed finite directed graph with no loops and with at most one edge on each unordered pair of vertices. Give its vertices independent uniform labels $Y_x\in\mathbb F_p$, and put $$I_G(Y)=\prod_{x\to y\in E(G)}W(Y_y-Y_x),\qquad e_G=|E(G)|.$$ Then $$\label{eq:tuple-graph-mean}
 \mathbb E I_G=c_p^{e_G}+O_G(p^{-4\delta}).$$ If the vertices are partitioned into two classes and every edge joins different classes, regard $I_G$ as a kernel between their uniform label spaces. Its associated operator satisfies $$\label{eq:tuple-graph-operator}
 \|I_G-c_p^{e_G}\|_{L^2\to L^2}=O_G(p^{-\delta}).$$ Both bounds are uniform in $l$.

The map $g\mapsto\|g\|_{l,\mathcal P}$ is a seminorm on the real functions on $X_{\mathcal P}$. It satisfies $$|\mathbb E g|\le\|g\|_{l,\mathcal P},
 \qquad
 \|\mathbb E_Bg\|_{l,\mathcal P\setminus B}
 =\|\mathbb E_Bg\|_{l,\mathcal P}
 \le\|g\|_{l,\mathcal P}.$$ Translations of the input are isometries. Moreover, if $D$ is a positive integer coprime to every prime of $\mathcal P$, then for any $a'\in X_{\mathcal P}$, $$\left\|g\bigl(\lambda(D)^{-1}(\,\cdot-a')\bigr)
       \right\|_{l,\mathcal P}
 =\|g\|_{lD,\mathcal P}.
 \label{eq:lift-transport}$$ Here the affine change is interpreted separately at each prime.

Let $\mathcal P$ be any finite set of primes, $Q\ge1$, and let $I$ be a consecutive integer interval with $|I|\ge10Q^6$. For every integer $r\ge1$, every $\epsilon>0$, every function $f:I\to\mathbb C$, and every subfamily $\mathcal F\subseteq\{B\subseteq\mathcal P:q_B\le Q\}$, $$\left\|\sum_{B\in\mathcal F}f_B\right\|_{2r}
 \le C_{r,\epsilon}\|f\|_\infty Q^\epsilon.
 \label{eq:lift-moments}$$ The constant is independent of the prime set, the interval and its location, the function, and the subfamily.

Uniformly for $Y\ge2$, $0\le x\le Y$, integers $b\ge1$, and reduced residue classes $c$ modulo $b$, $$\label{eq:prime-progression}
 \sum_{\substack{n\le x\\n\equiv c\pmod b}}\Lambda'(n)
 =\frac{x}{\varphi(b)}
   +O\bigl(Y^{31/32}\log^2(2bY)\bigr).$$ For a nonreduced class modulo $b>1$, the sum is $O(\log b)$ and has zero main term.

For fixed $k\ge2$ and $0<\rho<1$, there is a constant $c_m=c_m(k,\rho)\in(0,1/4)$ with the following property. If $Y$ is sufficiently large, $I\subseteq[1,Y]$ is an interval, and $f\in\mathbb R[x]$ has degree at most $k$ with coefficient $b_k$ of $x^k$, then $$\label{eq:prime-minor-arc}
 \left|\sum_{n\in I}\Lambda'(n)e(f(n))\right|
 \ll_{k,\rho}Y^{1-c_m},$$ provided $\theta=(k!)^2b_k$ has an approximation $a/q$ satisfying $$\label{eq:minor-rational-range}
 (a,q)=1,\qquad |\theta-a/q|\le q^{-2},\qquad
 Y^\rho\le q\le Y^{k-\rho}.$$

Suppose $(a,q)=1$, $q\ge2$, and $|\theta-a/q|\le q^{-2}$. For $R\ge1$ and $M\ge1$, $$\label{eq:rational-spacing}
 \sum_{1\le r\le R}\min(M,\|\theta r\|^{-1})
 \ll (R/q+1)\bigl(M+q\log(2q)\bigr).$$

Let $k\ge2$, and let $f\in\mathbb R[x]$ have degree at most $k$, with coefficient $b_k$ of $x^k$.*


*If $J$ is an interval of integers of length at most an integer $M\ge1$, and $m$ is a positive integer, then $$\label{eq:type-one-differencing}
 \left|\frac1M\sum_{n\in J}e(f(mn))\right|^{2^{k-1}}
 \ll_k\frac1{M^k}
 \sum_{|u_1|,\ldots,|u_{k-1}|<M}
 \min\bigl(M,\|k!b_km^ku_1\cdots u_{k-1}\|^{-1}\bigr).$$*


*Next let $A,B$ be positive integers, let $I\subseteq[1,Y]$ be an interval, and let $|a_m|,|d_n|\le1$. Set $$S=\sum_{\substack{A\le m<2A\\B\le n<2B}}
       a_md_n\mathbf 1_{\{mn\in I\}}e(f(mn)),\qquad
 \theta=(k!)^2b_k.$$ Then $$\label{eq:type-two-differencing}
 \left|\frac{S}{AB}\right|^{2^{2k-1}}
 \ll_k\frac1{A^kB^k}
 \sum_{\substack{|u_1|,\ldots,|u_k|<B\\
                  |v_1|,\ldots,|v_{k-1}|<A}}
 \min\bigl(A,\|\theta u_1\cdots u_kv_1\cdots v_{k-1}\|^{-1}\bigr).$$ Zero shifts are included in both sums.

For the data and ranges of Proposition <a href="#prop:pair-cancellation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:pair-cancellation">18</a>, $$\label{eq:kernel-comparison}
 \sup_{\alpha\in\mathbb R/\mathbb Z}|S_0(\alpha)-S_1(\alpha)|
 \ll H^{-\gamma/2}.$$

Let $N\ge1$, $A\subseteq[N]$, $s\bmod M_0$, and let $I\subseteq[N]$ be a nonempty consecutive interval. Let $B\subseteq\mathcal P_N$, write $q_B=\prod_{p\in B}p$, $D=M_0q_B$, and $\mathcal S=\mathcal P_N\setminus B$. Fix the coordinates indexed by $B$ of $\mathcal L_{I,\mathcal P_N,Q}f_s$ at $b=(b_p)_{p\in B}$, obtaining $g_I:X_{\mathcal S}\to\mathbb R$. Let $a'\bmod D$ be the unique residue satisfying $a'\equiv s\pmod{M_0}$ and $a'\equiv b_p\pmod p$ for $p\in B$. Then every frequency $\xi$ on $X_{\mathcal S}$ with $q(\xi)q_B\le Q$ satisfies $$\label{eq:residue-specialization}
 \widehat{g_I}(\xi)=\frac{D}{|I|}
       \sum_{\substack{x\in I\\x\equiv a'\pmod D}}
                       \mathbf1_A(x)e(-x\xi).$$

There is a constant $C_1$ such that for every sufficiently large $N$ and $1\le l\le N^\zeta$, $$\label{eq:descent-recurrence}
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal Y_l(N)\le {}&C_1N^{-\sigma}+(1+u_N)\biggl[
 (1-\rho_l)\mathcal Y_{lM_0}(n_\varnothing)\\
 &\hspace{12mm}+\rho_l
 \sum_{\substack{\varnothing\ne B\subseteq\mathcal P_N\\q_B\le N^\tau}}
       \eta_{l,B}\mathcal Y_{lD_B}(n_B)\biggr],\\
 u_N={}&(1+M_0^kN^{k\tau-1})^d-1=o(1).
 \end{split}$$ The constant and the sufficiently-large threshold are independent of $l$.
